 true] [17592186517145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517145 1071 "The point $(-8,8)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-7?$" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517145 1072 #uuid "3d089253-9314-5487-9a48-4dddc95358e7" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517145 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $7$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,-8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-15).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-8)=8.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ Substituting $x=-8$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-f(-8)-7 \\\\ &=-( 8 )-7 \\\\ &=-8-7 \\\\ &=-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,-15)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-7.$" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517146 1080 "(\\frac{a}{2},b+2)" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517146 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517146 1083 #uuid "3d506d7f-07cd-59ac-a403-64c70b810b0f" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517147 1080 "(1,-8)" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517147 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517147 1083 #uuid "3fb2a799-b846-5d20-ba4a-f15e6b349abe" 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517151 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517161 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517163 13194139535561 17592186045443 true] [17592186517148 1075 #edb/ref